{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310775,"nonce":"cy8Nxn6TIsfHlltzmtI5CZiiDyo=","title":"SOLVED a3432810: the 2x decode bimodality / non-completion of 7B on a busy Mac was the model mapping being EVICTED (cheapest reclaim ...","message":"That came from an uncontrolled comparison. Controlled, at the pressure where we\nfailed, **llama.cpp fails the same way** (0/1, 240s timeout, never finished\nloading). There was never a rayzor-specific cliff.\n\nThe mmap'd model is CLEAN FILE-BACKED memory = the kernel's cheapest reclaim\ntarget. Under pressure it gets dropped mid-run and every re-fault stalls a\ndecode step. That is the 2x bimodality: model resident -\u003e ~14 tok/s, partly\nevicted -\u003e ~7. `runtime/src/haxe_sys.rs` already documented this and already\nhad the fix (mlock), but `decide_mlock` returned false UNCONDITIONALLY on\nmacOS, so it never ran on the platform being measured.\n\nEvery macOS HOST signal is unusable, and that part of the old reasoning was\nright — do not try to revive them:\n  - `vm.swapusage.xsu_used` is monotonic history, **89% used on an idle GREEN\n    box**, so `leaning_on_swap` fires permanently. THIS IS THE TRAP: deleting\n    the `is_macos` bail alone would wire on every run.\n  - free pages sit near zero by design.\n  - `kern.memorystatus_vm_pressure_level` reads NORMAL at load time on the very\n    run that then stalls — a load-time check cannot see it coming.\n**The signal that works is the MAPPING'S SHARE OF RAM.** Wire in the band\n`[total/8, total/2]`. The old \"wiring buys nothing (86.7 vs 87.2 tok/s)\" A/B\nwas a 770MB Llama-1B = 4.8% of RAM, which the host absorbs either way; it was\nnever evidence about a 4GB model.\n\n| | result |\n|---|---|\n| unwired (old default) | 0/2 finished a 24-tok run in 120s |\n| wired | 2/2 at 11.0 / 11.6 tok/s (interleaved) |\n| llama.cpp | 0/1 in 240s |\n| quiet box, wired vs not | 10.22 vs 10.17 tok/s (stddev 3.8/3.3, n=6) = FREE |\n| no regression | Qwen 93.5 tok/s (below band, unwired); Mistral quiet 18.1, ttft 0.82s |\nCost moves to a ONE-TIME load: faulting+wiring 4GB against a hot 7GB set took\n35-186s, but always completed.\n\n`nue/bench/ballast.py GB SECONDS churn` — self-releasing.\n**PASSIVE BALLAST DOES NOT WORK**: at 5.0 and even 8.5 GB macOS just swaps it\nout and holds `pressure=1` with \u003e=60% free (13.8 GB swap). Only `churn` mode\n(re-touching every page) sustains level 2. Stamp\n`kern.memorystatus_vm_pressure_level` + compressor MB BEFORE AND AFTER every\nrun — conditions drift a lot between reps.\n\n- Single 16-token runs are USELESS under pressure (same config swung 10.2-16.1\n  tok/s); ttft subtraction amplifies noise. Use \u003e=64 tokens, \u003e=5 reps.\n- A 3-rep A/B said wiring cost 20%; at n=6 it was 0%. Do not size an A/B by\n  the first difference you see.\n- `bench.sh` exported `RAYZOR_NO_PRELOAD_MMAP=0` on Darwin intending \"on\", but\n  the runtime gates on PRESENCE (whole `RAYZOR_NO_*` family does), so preload\n  was OFF while the banner said on. Fixed in the same commit.\n\n\"Wait until the box is quiet\" is a benchmarking stance, not a product one.\nManufacturing the pressure is what found a real, shipped fix.\nSee [[feedback_perf_measurement_protocol]], [[perf_anonymous_heap_attributed]].\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): SOLVED a3432810: decode bimodality = model mapping EVICTED. Pressure via `nue/bench/ballast.py N S churn` (passive does NOT work).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_7b_busy_laptop_pressure_robustness` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}